1
00:00:00,317 --> 00:00:01,884
What is backpropagation?

2
00:00:02,086 --> 00:00:04,792
It is the chain rule, organised so that one backward

3
00:00:04,653 --> 00:00:07,158
sweep gives the derivative of the error with respect

4
00:00:07,019 --> 00:00:09,323
to every weight in a network, all at once.

5
00:00:10,139 --> 00:00:12,164
Gradient descent takes the step.

6
00:00:12,336 --> 00:00:14,830
Backpropagation supplies the direction.

7
00:00:15,011 --> 00:00:17,761
Together they are how every neural network learns.

8
00:00:19,245 --> 00:00:21,538
Here is the obvious way to find a slope.

9
00:00:21,728 --> 00:00:24,189
Nudge one weight, run the whole network again,

10
00:00:24,273 --> 00:00:25,829
and see how the error moved.

11
00:00:26,036 --> 00:00:28,497
That is one full pass, for one weight.

12
00:00:29,370 --> 00:00:32,165
A thousand weights means a thousand and one passes.

13
00:00:32,367 --> 00:00:34,604
A billion weights means a billion and one.

14
00:00:34,811 --> 00:00:37,427
In nineteen seventy, Seppo Linnainmaa showed there

15
00:00:37,289 --> 00:00:39,872
is a better way, and in nineteen seventy-four Paul

16
00:00:39,733 --> 00:00:41,903
Werbos applied it to neural networks.

17
00:00:42,110 --> 00:00:43,443
One forward pass.

18
00:00:43,650 --> 00:00:44,994
One backward pass.

19
00:00:45,202 --> 00:00:46,468
Every derivative.

20
00:00:47,912 --> 00:00:50,305
It rests on one fact from calculus.

21
00:00:50,507 --> 00:00:53,547
If y depends on u, and u depends on x, then a small

22
00:00:53,408 --> 00:00:56,739
nudge in x is scaled once by d u by d x, and again

23
00:00:56,600 --> 00:00:57,889
by d y by d u.

24
00:00:57,750 --> 00:00:58,770
Rates multiply.

25
00:00:59,620 --> 00:01:00,953
Watch the nudge travel.

26
00:01:01,155 --> 00:01:03,994
It enters at x, is stretched by the first slope,

27
00:01:04,079 --> 00:01:05,200
then by the second.

28
00:01:05,377 --> 00:01:07,425
The total stretch is the product.

29
00:01:07,617 --> 00:01:09,608
A network is just a longer chain.

30
00:01:11,049 --> 00:01:13,721
Take the smallest network that has a hidden layer.

31
00:01:13,919 --> 00:01:17,897
Input x, weight w one, a sigmoid, weight w two,

32
00:01:18,015 --> 00:01:20,096
output y hat, and a squared error.

33
00:01:20,280 --> 00:01:21,189
Two weights.

34
00:01:21,374 --> 00:01:22,484
Four stages.

35
00:01:23,306 --> 00:01:25,309
Forward pass, with numbers.

36
00:01:25,499 --> 00:01:30,493
x is 1.5, w one is 0.8, so z is 1.2.

37
00:01:30,700 --> 00:01:34,131
The sigmoid gives h equals 0.769.

38
00:01:34,338 --> 00:01:39,522
w two is -0.4, so y hat is -0.307.

39
00:01:39,718 --> 00:01:41,006
The target is 1.

40
00:01:41,213 --> 00:01:43,696
The error is 1.709.

41
00:01:44,514 --> 00:01:45,646
Now backwards.

42
00:01:45,848 --> 00:01:48,007
The error depends on y hat through the square,

43
00:01:48,136 --> 00:01:53,387
so d L by d y hat is two times y hat minus y: -2.615.

44
00:01:54,245 --> 00:01:57,576
y hat is w two times h, so the slope with respect

45
00:01:57,437 --> 00:02:01,292
to w two is that number times h: -2.01.

46
00:02:01,472 --> 00:02:02,838
The first weight is done.

47
00:02:03,631 --> 00:02:06,303
To reach w one we must pass through h.

48
00:02:06,502 --> 00:02:09,532
The slope with respect to h is d L by d y hat,

49
00:02:09,683 --> 00:02:12,478
times w two: 1.046.

50
00:02:12,656 --> 00:02:13,821
Keep this number.

51
00:02:14,002 --> 00:02:16,262
Everything before h will reuse it.

52
00:02:17,090 --> 00:02:19,852
h is the sigmoid of z, and the sigmoid's slope

53
00:02:19,713 --> 00:02:23,569
is sigma times one minus sigma: here 0.178.

54
00:02:23,755 --> 00:02:25,524
z is w one times x.

55
00:02:25,385 --> 00:02:31,539
Multiply the chain: 1.046, times 0.178, times 1.5.

56
00:02:31,724 --> 00:02:35,244
d L by d w one is 0.279.

57
00:02:36,047 --> 00:02:38,497
Two derivatives, one backward sweep.

58
00:02:38,696 --> 00:02:41,145
Hand them to gradient descent with eta equal to

59
00:02:41,006 --> 00:02:47,238
0.5: w one becomes 0.66, w two becomes 0.605, and

60
00:02:47,100 --> 00:02:50,855
the error falls from 1.709 to 0.312.

61
00:02:52,317 --> 00:02:53,951
Notice what we reused.

62
00:02:54,153 --> 00:02:57,049
The slope at h was computed once, and every weight

63
00:02:56,910 --> 00:02:58,968
upstream of h multiplied by it.

64
00:02:59,176 --> 00:03:01,647
In a wide layer, hundreds of weights share the

65
00:03:01,508 --> 00:03:02,317
same number.

66
00:03:03,132 --> 00:03:05,816
Call that shared quantity delta: the slope of the

67
00:03:05,677 --> 00:03:07,914
error with respect to a layer's inputs.

68
00:03:08,112 --> 00:03:10,561
Delta for one layer is the next layer's delta,

69
00:03:10,667 --> 00:03:13,128
pulled back through its weights, times the sigmoid

70
00:03:12,989 --> 00:03:13,574
slope.

71
00:03:13,781 --> 00:03:16,465
And the gradient of a weight matrix is simply delta

72
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:17,793
times the layer's input.

73
00:03:17,978 --> 00:03:18,998
Three lines.

74
00:03:19,183 --> 00:03:20,092
Any depth.

75
00:03:21,453 --> 00:03:23,099
Here is the proof you can run.

76
00:03:23,270 --> 00:03:26,154
Take the backward-sweep gradient, and the nudge-and-remeasure

77
00:03:26,015 --> 00:03:27,973
gradient, on the same surface.

78
00:03:28,167 --> 00:03:31,252
The arrows coincide, to six decimal places.

79
00:03:32,745 --> 00:03:36,098
In nineteen eighty-six, Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams

80
00:03:35,959 --> 00:03:38,353
published this in Nature, and showed that the hidden

81
00:03:38,214 --> 00:03:40,361
layers learn useful features on their own.

82
00:03:40,552 --> 00:03:43,057
Three years later, Yann LeCun's group trained a

83
00:03:42,918 --> 00:03:45,445
network by backpropagation to read handwritten

84
00:03:45,307 --> 00:03:47,678
postcodes for the United States Postal Service:

85
00:03:47,829 --> 00:03:51,249
9298 digits, about one percent error.

86
00:03:52,078 --> 00:03:53,467
Here is a small version.

87
00:03:53,669 --> 00:03:56,754
600 noisy eight-by-eight digits, a network with

88
00:03:56,615 --> 00:03:59,154
one hidden layer, and nothing but the three lines

89
00:03:59,015 --> 00:03:59,834
you just saw.

90
00:04:00,042 --> 00:04:02,725
It starts at 61 percent.

91
00:04:02,586 --> 00:04:06,241
After 40 passes through the data it reads 91 percent

92
00:04:05,544 --> 00:04:06,241
correctly.

93
00:04:07,681 --> 00:04:10,387
Today nobody writes these derivatives by hand.

94
00:04:10,577 --> 00:04:13,060
Frameworks record the forward computation as a

95
00:04:12,921 --> 00:04:15,493
graph and run the backward sweep automatically.

96
00:04:15,700 --> 00:04:18,428
The algorithm is unchanged, from two weights to

97
00:04:18,289 --> 00:04:19,477
hundreds of billions.

98
00:04:20,299 --> 00:04:22,089
But the chain rule has a cost.

99
00:04:22,265 --> 00:04:25,071
Each sigmoid layer multiplies the backward signal

100
00:04:24,932 --> 00:04:26,176
by at most a quarter.

101
00:04:26,372 --> 00:04:29,089
Six layers deep, the gradient reaching the first

102
00:04:28,950 --> 00:04:31,198
weights is thousands of times smaller than at the

103
00:04:31,059 --> 00:04:31,779
output.

104
00:04:31,975 --> 00:04:34,346
That is the vanishing gradient, and it is why the

105
00:04:34,207 --> 00:04:37,471
next lessons exist: better activations, residual

106
00:04:37,332 --> 00:04:39,145
connections, normalisation.

107
00:04:40,609 --> 00:04:42,701
So, what is backpropagation?

108
00:04:42,903 --> 00:04:45,430
The chain rule, run backwards through a network,

109
00:04:45,525 --> 00:04:47,896
giving every weight's derivative in one sweep.

110
00:04:48,734 --> 00:04:49,297
When?

111
00:04:49,476 --> 00:04:51,546
Linnainmaa, nineteen seventy.

112
00:04:51,753 --> 00:04:53,979
Werbos, nineteen seventy-four.

113
00:04:54,164 --> 00:04:57,751
Rumelhart, Hinton and Williams, nineteen eighty-six.

114
00:04:57,947 --> 00:04:59,481
How is it used in A.I.?

115
00:04:59,677 --> 00:05:02,695
Every training step of every network: forward pass,

116
00:05:02,769 --> 00:05:05,061
backward pass, gradient descent step.

117
00:05:05,269 --> 00:05:07,807
Next: vectors and the dot product.
